":"verifier_lab_execution_spine","through_axiom_ids":["AX-5"]},{"basis":"derived_from_violated_axiom_citations","component_id":"verifier_lab_kernel","through_axiom_ids":["AX-5"]},{"basis":"derived_from_violated_axiom_citations","component_id":"voice_to_doctrine_self_improvement_loop","through_axiom_ids":["AX-12"]},{"basis":"derived_from_violated_axiom_citations","component_id":"world_model_projection_drift_control_room","through_axiom_ids":["AX-5"]}],"failure_modes":[],"id":"AP-3","kind":"anti_principle","plain":"Reading counts, handles, passing validators or evidence classes as a measure of product maturity, release readiness or whole-system correctness.","principles":[],"reader":{"analogy":{"boundary":"The picture only covers hidden gaps in a tally; it says nothing about whether the lines that do report are running well.","maps":[{"analogy":"the forty on-time trains read as the whole network being fine","doctrine":"a count or tally read as the standing of the whole"},{"analogy":"a line with no data on the board that could be closed","doctrine":"a part with no supporting evidence, which should hold the whole down"},{"analogy":"the network being only as good as its worst line","doctrine":"the status of the whole being its weakest part"}],"text":"A station board says forty trains ran on time, and someone concludes the whole network is running well, although several lines have no data on the board and could be closed."},"basis":"authored_reader_enrichment","counterexample":"A progress page reports that ninety checks pass and calls the system ready for release, while several required parts have no supporting evidence and the gaps are averaged away.","does_not_prove":"Refusing to read counts and passing validators as a maturity score adds no support to any part.","enforced_in_component_ids":["self_ignorance_coverage_ledger","batch12_release_claim_language_gate"],"example":{"component_ids":["self_ignorance_coverage_ledger"],"text":"The coverage ledger counts how many declared parts actually exist and includes a test case that fails if a missing entry is read as covered, so its tally reports a bounded count."},"governs":"Progress and readiness reports that summarise many parts with one number.","plain":"The mistake of reading one summary number, such as how many checks passed, as if it said the whole thing is finished or ready, when parts nobody measured could still be failing.","potential_misread":"That more passing checks always means more readiness, when repeating a check that already passed adds nothing.","refuses":"Treating counts, names, passing validators or evidence-class names as product maturity, release readiness or correctness of the whole system.","requires":"Scoring the whole by its weakest part, counting a part with no evidence as blocked, and raising standing only by new coverage, a higher score on a part or a missing negative case.","source_ref":"core/doctrine_enrichment.json","why_it_matters":"Counting passes as a maturity score treats everything unmeasured as passed, so a project can look ready for release while a required piece was never tested."},"rests_on":[],"source_ref":"ANTI_PRINCIPLES.md::AP-3","statement":"| ID | Violated axioms | Rejected failure shape |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| AP-3 Rank-as-product-score | AX-5, AX-12 | Reading counts, handles, passing validators or evidence classes as a measure of product maturity, release readiness or whole-system correctness. |","statement_sha256":"17517c8a13fbf7ae3c8fa020a6db576d9e8da733c442717bc144a0b797a5e5a4","title":"Rank-as-product-score","violates":["AX-5","AX-12"]},"schema_version":"plectis_component_http_v1","snapshot_revision":"45be1deb333ad57cd9ae093229455d0a52d3bf23a9064cde09f1a6e16f4c7044"}